From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Renaud Deraison <renaud@secl.io>
Date: Mon, 2 Nov 2026 18:14:37 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] net: an inherited AF_UNIX SOCK_DGRAM socket as a backend

host_dev_name "fd:<N>" (':' is never in an interface name) takes the
inherited descriptor N, a connected AF_UNIX SOCK_DGRAM socket, instead of
opening a tap: one Ethernet frame per datagram, without the virtio-net
header, which is dropped on the way out and zero-filled on the way in. No
offloads are offered over it, so frames are whole and checksummed.

A datagram arrives whole or not at all, and there is no connection to
lose: the host side can restart while it keeps its end of a socketpair
open, and nothing here notices. Sends that fail because the peer is full
or gone (EAGAIN, ENOBUFS, ECONNREFUSED, ENOTCONN) drop the frame and count
it in tap_write_fails without logging each one; the TX queue never stalls.
A frame longer than the guest's receive buffers is dropped whole and
counted in tap_read_fails (recvmsg, MSG_TRUNC), never handed over cut; a
header with no frame sends nothing. Socket buffers are asked for 4 MiB
(capped by the kernel).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
